A range loop counts through numbers.

`to` stops before the end value:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop 0 to 5 |value|:
        io.line([: [value]])
    ;
]

This produces `0`, `1`, `2`, `3` and `4`.

Use `to &` when the end value should be included:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop 0 to & 5 |value|:
        io.line([: [value]])
    ;
]

This also produces `5`.

### Start from zero

You can leave out the first `0`.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop to 5 |value|:
        io.line([: [value]])
    ;
]

### Change the step

Use `by` to skip between values:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop 0 to 10 by 2 |value|:
        io.line([: [value]])
    ;
]

This produces `0`, `2`, `4`, `6` and `8`.

### Count downward

The direction comes from the bounds. You do not need a `reverse` keyword.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop 5 to 0 |value|:
        io.line([: [value]])
    ;
]

Any range using `F16`, `F32` or `F64` needs an explicit step:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop 0.0 to 1.0 by 0.1 |amount|:
        io.line([: [amount]])
    ;
]

The value binding uses the selected range type. The optional position binding
always has type `I32`, even when the range counter uses another exact type.

Fixed-width bounds and steps use the exact numeric domain rules. All omitted
zero and default one values materialise in the selected counter type. Invalid
steps and counter updates produce implicit failure through the enclosing
handler or function contract. Ranges run on HTML-JS; HTML-Wasm rejects
recoverable range failure with a capability diagnostic. See @../progress
(Progress) for current support.
